WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.825
Your grandfather navigated with a compass, a watch and a lead line.

2
00:00:05.075 --> 00:00:08.250
You have a screen that shows a little boat on a chart.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.450
That’s a wonderful thing, right up to the moment it goes blank.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.950
So today we learn what the electronics really tell you, and where each one can fool you.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.100
Start with GPS.

6
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:25.350
It fixes your position from satellite signals, typically within a few meters.

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.075
A correction system called WAAS, available across North America, makes it better still.

8
00:00:32.325 --> 00:00:36.250
It also gives you speed over ground and course over ground.

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.300
Where do those come from?

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.675
From how your position changes.

11
00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:43.775
Not from the compass.

12
00:00:44.025 --> 00:00:48.250
So sitting still at the dock, your GPS heading means nothing.

13
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.450
Set the receiver’s datum to match your chart: WGS eighty-four, which matches the North American Datum on the chart.

14
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.325
And GPS can fail, lose power, or be jammed.

15
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:04.100
The old Loran backup was shut down in twenty ten.

16
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.000
Your backup now is you: dead reckoning, a paper chart and a compass.
